Before implementing the new features, 
the existing pytest suite was executed to confirm that the project was working correctly and the baseline result was saved. After implementing the features, the full pytest suite was executed again to verify that the new functionality worked correctly without breaking existing behavior. New tests were added for due dates and overdue filtering, including creating tasks with valid due dates, rejecting invalid date formats, detecting overdue tasks, ensuring completed tasks are not marked overdue, updating due dates, clearing due dates, and filtering overdue tasks. Additional tests were added for tags, including creating tasks with tags, rejecting blank tags, removing duplicate tags, updating tags, preserving tags during unrelated updates, and filtering tasks by tag. Manual browser checks were performed to verify creating tasks with and without due dates, displaying overdue indicators, using the overdue filter, displaying tags, filtering by tags, and keeping tags after updates. Before refactoring, the application supported the original task creation, viewing, updating, deleting, and filtering features. After refactoring, the application supports optional due dates, dynamic overdue detection, overdue filtering, task tags, and tag filtering while maintaining the previous functionality. Break testing was performed by intentionally removing due date validation and tag filtering logic, which caused the tests test_create_task_with_invalid_due_date_format_returns_422 and test_filter_by_tag_returns_only_matching_tasks to fail, confirming that the tests successfully detected broken behavior.